    <div class="row mb-4">
        <div class="col-12 col-lg-9">
            <h1 class="article-title">How to Maintain Response Body on a 204 No Content HTTP Response?</h1>

            <div class="text-end mb-5">
                <a href="/questions/java" class="category-link py-1 ms-2 mb-1">
                    <span class="px-2">java</span>
                </a>
                <a href="/questions/spring" class="category-link py-1 ms-2 mb-1">
                    <span class="px-2">spring</span>
                </a>
                <a href="/questions/spring-mvc" class="category-link py-1 ms-2 mb-1">
                    <span class="px-2">spring-mvc</span>
                </a>
                <a href="/questions/httpresponse" class="category-link py-1 ms-2 mb-1">
                    <span class="px-2">httpresponse</span>
                </a>
            </div>

            <main class="mb-5" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/TechArticle">
                <section>
                    <h2 class="h3">Question</h2>
                    <div class="ms-2">
                        <p>What techniques can be used to retain or manage Response Body data when sending a 204 No Content HTTP response?</p>
                        <div class="code-block">
                            <i class="copy-button copy fas fa-copy fa-lg" onclick="copyToClipboard(event)"></i>
                            <span class="copy-button copied font-sm" style="display: none">Copied</span>
                            <pre class="mb-0"><span class="hljs-keyword">None</span> needed <span class="hljs-keyword">for</span> a <span class="hljs-number">204</span> response.</pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <div class="text-center my-4">
                    <a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/0134685997" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" class="d-none d-lg-block"><img src="/images/ads/ads1.png" class="img-fluid" alt="Recommended Java programming book"></a>
                    <a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/0134685997" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" class="d-lg-none"><img src="/images/ads/ads2.png" class="img-fluid" alt="Recommended Java programming book"></a>
                </div>

                <section>
                    <h2 class="h3">Answer</h2>
                    <div class="ms-2">
                        <p>In HTTP, a 204 No Content response indicates that the server successfully processed the request, and there is no content to send back to the client. Traditionally, this response type does not include a message body, hence &#x27;No Content.&#x27; However, if you need to convey information despite the 204 status, there are several strategies you can use to implement this without breaking the HTTP protocol rules.</p>
                        <p class="inline-resource">For broader software-development habits, <a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/0135957052" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener">The Pragmatic Programmer, 20th Anniversary Edition</a> is a useful companion.</p>
                        <div class="code-block">
                            <i class="copy-button copy fas fa-copy fa-lg" onclick="copyToClipboard(event)"></i>
                            <span class="copy-button copied font-sm" style="display: none">Copied</span>
                            <pre class="mb-0"><span class="hljs-keyword">None</span> required, <span class="hljs-keyword">as</span> the <span class="hljs-number">204</span> response forbids a body.</pre>
                        </div>
                        <h4 class="font-weight-600">Causes</h4>
                        <ul>
                            <li>The HTTP 204 status code explicitly indicates that there is no content to return.</li>
                            <li>HTTP specifications dictate that a 204 response must not include a message body.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <h4 class="font-weight-600">Solutions</h4>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Use a different status code (e.g., 200 OK) that supports sending content if you need to include a body.</li>
                            <li>Utilize HTTP headers to provide needed information instead of a message body.</li>
                            <li>In some scenarios, consider client-side caching techniques where further requests can retrieve data separately.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p class="inline-resource">For a useful code-quality reference, <a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/0132350882" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener">Clean Code</a> is a useful companion.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section>
                    <h2 class="h3">Common Mistakes</h2>
                    <div class="ms-2">
                        <p class="mb-1"><b>Mistake:</b> Sending a response body with a 204 No Content status code.</p>
                        <p><b>Solution: </b> Change the response code to 200 OK to include a response body.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="ms-2">
                        <p class="mb-1"><b>Mistake:</b> Using 204 response where client needs additional data after an operation.</p>
                        <p><b>Solution: </b> Use 200 OK and return the relevant data as needed.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <div class="text-center my-4">
                    <a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/0134685997" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" class="d-none d-lg-block"><img src="/images/ads/ads1.png" class="img-fluid" alt="Recommended Java programming book"></a>
                    <a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/0134685997" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" class="d-lg-none"><img src="/images/ads/ads2.png" class="img-fluid" alt="Recommended Java programming book"></a>
                </div>

                <section>
                    <h2 class="h3">Key Topics</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>HTTP 204 No Content</li>
                        <li>Response Body handling</li>
                        <li>HTTP status codes</li>
                        <li>REST API response management</li>
                        <li>HTTP response best practices</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
            </main>

